+Construction and verification
+-----------------------------
+Each graph starts with one of two symmetric integer templates of the same
+size: +/-{1,...,r}, whose triangle count is q*r*(r-1)/2, or
++/-{1,3,...,2r-1}, which has no additive triples by parity.  The modulus is
+larger than every possible three-term integer combination.  Multiplication by
+a random unit modulo q is a vertex relabelling, so it carries the known count
+without changing it.  Template bits, moduli, radii, multipliers, graph order,
+and step order all vary with the seed.  Generation never runs the verifier.
 
+The verifier knows none of the template bits or multipliers.  For each graph it
+counts ordered pairs (s,t) for which t-s is another connection step and returns
+q times that number divided by six.  Table I's synthetic-grid triangle values
+appear to be twice the count implied by the paper's own triangle definition
+and divide-by-six Algorithm 2; this module follows the definition and algorithm.
 
