codex
Built a self-contained Track B generator based on the paper’s native eight-neighbour image graphs and triangle-counting kernel ([paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/1708.06866)).

Deliverables:

- [gen_1708_06866.py](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1708.06866/gen_1708_06866.py)
- [selftest_report.json](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1708.06866/selftest_report.json)
- [README.md](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1708.06866/README.md)
- [llm_loop_transcript.jsonl](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1708.06866/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- [g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1708.06866/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl)
- [g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1708.06866/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl)

Local verification passes all G1–G9 gates:

- G1: 12/12 planted certificates
- G4: 0/200,000 structure-aware guesses
- G6: six attacks at 0/8; reference algorithm 8/8
- Reference cost: 7,417,830 operations, 1.458 seconds mean
- G8: 100/100 relabelling checks; 20/20 distinct instances
- G9: 278 characters, 41 atoms, 288 intended operations

The result is not release-ready because OpenRouter returned HTTP 403 “Key limit exceeded” for every bare and G9 redraw. Those calls remain recorded as errors—not model failures—so there is currently no valid oracle-hardening evidence. No `REJECTED.md` was created because G, H, and V pass; renewed OpenRouter budget is the sole blocker.
